 #:seon.task{:name "an-edit-rewrites-every-definition-below-it"
             :plan "A publication or adoption rewrites `:seon.fn/form-span` for every declaration below an edit, because spans are absolute byte offsets in the file. `:seon.program/analyzed-source-digest` no longer moves with them (`seon.fn/analyzed-source-digest` digests the declaration's own source and its namespace's resolver context). Measured on default 2026-10-15: publication tx 536873082 rewrote form-span on 566 declarations for 30 changed sources; adoption tx 536872614 on 1,145 for 41. Each retraction costs Datahike a sibling-leaf recount (`docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-15/bulk-tx.md`). Option (a), measured: derive the span at read time from the file text and the declaration's `:seon.fn/source` (all 10,334 declarations on default occur exactly once in their file; deriving every span of the program took 542 ms with file reads, one 324 KB file about 1 ms per declaration naively) and stop storing it; the readers are `my.program` write-back, `seon.effect` attribution, `seon.program/declaration-at`, `seon.cluster.source` merge spans, `seon.turn.batch`, `seon.sci.eval` ordering and `seon.render.test`. Option (b): store the span relative to the previous declaration (an insertion rewrites one neighbour), every reader sums. Done when editing one defn in a 100-definition file writes O(changed definitions) program datoms, with a regression on that count. Read `seon.fn/analyze-forms` and `seon.fn/same-indexed-entity?`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "backticked-docstring-references-have-no-index",
            :plan
            "CLAUDE.md says a backticked fully qualified symbol or keyword in a docstring is a reference the index resolves into the subject's context, one resolving to nothing a problem on that var. No producer exists: seon.fn indexes :seon/tasks, :seon/must-read and :seon/see-also metadata (task-links) and analyzer `:seon.fn/references`, never docstring spans. Done when docstring backtick spans are indexed as reference edges with dangling ones reported, or the law is corrected. Read `seon.fn/task-links` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/context-design/reference-kinds-notes.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "batched-analysis-uses-one-namespace-row-per-run"
  :plan "`seon.fn/runtime-analysis-batch` builds one ns form from the first row of a run of consecutive requests (partition-by on namespace name), so later requests with a different row are analyzed under the first. Probe (JVM, default's database value): two requests in namespace probe.a, row 1 x -> clojure.string, row 2 x -> clojure.set, forms (x/upper-case \"a\") and (x/union #{1} #{2}). Batched: usages [clojure.string upper-case] and [clojure.string union] (the second is wrong); analyzed one by one: [clojure.string upper-case] and [clojure.set union]. Correct: a run splits where the namespace row changes (not only the name). Proof: seon.fn-test with those two requests asserts [clojure.string upper-case] and [clojure.set union]. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/alias-design.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-renamed-refer-drops-its-call-edge"
  :plan "runtime-require-specs (fn.clj:814) emits :refer [target-name] only and drops the local name of a :rename, so a renamed refer is invisible to analysis. Probe (probe.alias7): (require '[clojure.set :refer [union] :rename {union upper-case}]) then (defn r2 [] (upper-case ...)) stores calls [defn] with no edge to clojure.set/union. Correct: the analysis ns form carries :rename so the call resolves to clojure.set/union. Proof: that pair of forms stores clojure.set/union in r2's calls. Read `seon.fn/runtime-require-specs` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/s5-admission/alias-design.md`."}
